# pf-killswitch.conf — leak-proof kill-switch for qbittorrent/sonarr/radarr.
#
# gluetun's whole selling point was: if the tunnel drops, the torrent client
# loses ALL connectivity rather than falling back to your real IP.
#
# This used to be a ruleset loaded INSIDE the vpn jail. Confirmed live (2026-09-14)
# that doesn't work at all: FreeBSD's pf is not vnet/jail-aware -- `pfctl`
# inside even a VNET jail with every allow.* flag thrown at it fails with
# "DIOCADDRULE: Operation not permitted". So this is appended to the HOST's
# own /etc/pf.conf instead (after the jail-NAT rule in pf-nat.conf.tmpl —
# pf.conf requires nat/rdr before filter rules), and enforces the kill-switch
# at the one place that's actually allowed to filter: the host's WAN NIC.
#
# The logic: qbittorrent/sonarr/radarr's defaultrouter is the vpn jail's own
# IP (set by create_vpn_stack), never the bridge gateway directly. As long as
# the WireGuard tunnel is up (confirmed live -- with `Table` left at
# wg-quick's default, AllowedIPs=0.0.0.0/0 makes it install 0.0.0.0/1 +
# 128.0.0.0/1 as the vpn jail's OWN most-specific routes), their forwarded
# traffic is captured into wg0 by the vpn jail's own routing table before it
# ever reaches this rule. If the tunnel drops, those two routes vanish with
# the interface and the vpn jail's only remaining path for that traffic is
# back out its plain bridge gateway route -- unencapsulated, still carrying
# the confined jails' own source IPs, re-emitted on the vpn jail's OWN
# bridge-facing interface (e0a_<jail>, the host-visible half of its epair --
# NOT the shared bridge macro: confirmed live that net.link.bridge.pfil_member
# has to be considered, and that filtering the shared bridge interface can't
# distinguish "qbittorrent legitimately reaching the vpn jail" from "vpn jail
# leaking qbittorrent's packet back onto the bridge" since both are the same
# (source, dest-family) pair on the same wire -- only the PER-JAIL epair leg
# tells them apart). Destination is explicitly exempted so the confined
# jails can still reach the vpn jail itself (the one legitimate hop) --
# confirmed live this rule needs NO "in"/"out" direction qualifier (adding
# one broke the match entirely against this address-family/list syntax).

block drop quick on e0a_{{VPN_JAIL_NAME}} from { {{VPN_CONFINED_IPS}} } to ! {{VPN_JAIL_IP}}
